.TH OPENOS 1 "" "" "mcos Manual"
.SH NAME
openos \- run an OpenComputers program
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B openos
.I program
.RI [ args ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B openos
runs a program written for OpenComputers' OpenOS on mcos, as wine runs a
Windows program on Linux: in a machine of its own, with OpenComputers'
.BR component ,
.B computer
and
.B unicode
libraries, and OpenOS's own libraries (event, term, keyboard, shell,
filesystem, serialization, text, sides, colors, uuid, robot), ported.
.I program
is a path, with or without its
.BR .lua .
.PP
The gpu draws on the program's terminal (its screen, a tier's colors and
size), and the keyboard and mouse are the terminal's (key_down, key_up,
touch, drag, drop, scroll, clipboard). Devices are components by an adapter:
the EEPROM (eeprom), a redstone card (redstone), a network card (modem, over
UDP), a golem's robotics (robot). Other devices aren't components. Ctrl+C is
OpenOS's: an interrupted signal; Ctrl+Alt+C ends the program.
.PP
Whatever OpenOS had that
.B openos
doesn't is an error saying so, never something else done instead: a
library (process, thread, internet...), a method (a gpu's video memory, a
robot's tanks).
.SH EXIT STATUS
What the program's
.B os.exit
gave, or the integer it returned; 0 when it ends; 1 when it fails, its error
on standard error; 127 when the program isn't there.
.SH NOTES
mcos's commands run where OpenOS's would:
.B os.execute
and
.B shell.execute
run mcos's
.BR sh .
A modem message of one string is that string, so mcos programs (nc -u) talk
with it. A golem's inventory is its hand, one slot. A redstone face's input
is its level, the card's own output included.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR lua (1)
